{"t":1791383397,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"9a241221-52b8-4d83-beda-3da1d8f2eac6","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-07T08:57:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 070857\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n457 AM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nTropical Depression 9 has become Tropical Storm Isaias.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Quiescent weather expected through the end of the week.\n\n- 2) Increasing rain potential Saturday night through Monday.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Quiescent weather expected through the end of \nthe week.\n\nHigh pressure moves offshore today turning the winds out of the\nsouth. This will yield a milder day with temps climbing back \ninto the 70s. Temps warm up to near 80 Thu before the passage \nof another dry cold front. Temps cool off slightly for the end \nof the week as weak high pressure of Canadian origin builds in. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Increased rain potential late this weekend into\nearly next week. \n\nThe main feature to watch in the extended portion of the forecast \nwill be Tropical Storm Isaias. Key steering features will be a \nridge in the western Atlantic, a trough/closed low over Texas, \nand eventually a trough ejecting from the northern Rockies. The \nNHC forecast has the remnants of this system moving into the \nTennessee and Ohio Valleys over the weekend, although there \nstill remains considerable uncertainty on the track and \nespecially forward speed of the system. Even despite this \nwestward position of the cyclone, moisture from both the Gulf of\nAmerica and Atlantic will likely be transported northward into \nthe Mid Atlantic. Rain will become increasingly likely Sun, \nalthough as mentioned, this timing could vary a bit. The remnant\nlow could then move eastward, potentially extending rain \nchances into Monday. At this juncture, the rain will be the main\nimpact from this system, but we will continue to monitor any \nchanging trends.\n\nIt will be important to stay tuned to the forecast over the next few \ndays, and monitor hurricanes.gov for updated details on Tropical \nStorm Isaias.\n\nDaytime temperatures will likely be seasonal through the weekend and \nearly next week but will take a dip when rain is the most \nwidespread. Meanwhile overnight lows will be mild thanks to \nincreasing clouds and dew points.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /08Z WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nVFR conditions are expected through the end of the week. Winds\nturn S'ly today as high pressure shifts offshore. A dry cold \nfront Thu will cause another wind shift to the west and \nNW into Fri.\n\nVFR conditions with light south to southeast winds are expected \nSaturday. Rain and low ceilings are possible late Saturday night \ninto Sunday as tropical moisture is drawn northward into the\narea from the Gulf of America.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nWinds are expected to strengthen today out of the south and \ncould flirt with marginal SCA conditions this evening. Currently\nfavoring an MWS given the short duration and marginal nature. A\ndry frontal passage Thu could bring another period of marginal \nSCA conditions Thu afternoon into Thu night.\n\nWinds will become southeasterly Sat into Sat night, likely \nremaining below SCA levels. Winds could increase on Sun, \ndepending on the evolution of the weather pattern ahead of a \nremnant tropical system (currently Tropical Storm Isaias) which\nwill likely be moving into the Tennessee or Ohio Valleys.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...ADS/CPB\nAVIATION...ADS/CPB\nMARINE...ADS/CPB\n"},"forecast":{"t":1791383335,"days":{"2026-10-07":{"hi":72,"lo":54,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-08":{"hi":78,"lo":52,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-09":{"hi":74,"lo":54,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026